  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCurrent activity over Indiana/western Ohio is expected to\ncontinue more of a southeast trajectory along the mixed-layer \nCAPE gradient into southern Ohio and central West Virginia, \nmainly as rain showers with a low probability for thunder. \nPerhaps, if clouds clear sufficiently, enough buoyancy could \ndevelop in Muskingum/Coshocton Counties and vicinity for a brief\nthreat of strong wind gusts from a wet microburst this \nafternoon, but confidence in such a threat remains low.\n\nCAMs continue to suggest that the next convective complex \ndevelops in western Ohio around 00Z, associated with a mid-level\nshortwave and a strengthening low-level jet. This activity \nwould then arrive in our eastern Ohio counties around 03Z or so \nand track across the region through sunrise. By that point, low-\nlevel buoyancy should be minimal, and thus severe risk should \nbe a secondary concern to the flash flood potential.\n\nVery efficient rainfall production is expected with any such storms.\nBehind a warm front, overall moisture levels will surge, with \nNBM/HREF suggesting precipitable water values climbing into the \n2 to 2.25 inch range. The higher end of that range, if actually \nobserved by our 12Z sounding, could be one of the highest such \nPWAT readings ever recorded (since 1952) at this upper air site.\nDeep saturation and impressively high warm-cloud depths of \naround 14kft are also noted, highlighting the expected tropical \nairmass. HREF progs suggest maximum hourly rainfall rates of 1.5\nto 1.75 inches are possible, which would exceed 1-hour flash \nflood guidance in counties from Pittsburgh on south. Considering\nthis and the favorable environment for excessive rain, a Flood \nWatch has been issued from midnight through 8AM Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nConfidence in what follows for Sunday and Sunday night drops\noff, as events will depend on the mesoscale setup left behind by\nthe overnight complex, most notably how much clearing/subsequent\ndestabilization can occur. While some stable air should limit\ncoverage and intensity of convection at least into early\nafternoon, some CAMs do suggest development of sufficient \nMLCAPE to refire convection during the late afternoon and\nevening, particularly south of Pittsburgh in the SPC day 2\nSlight Risk area. Here, a pocket of 25-30 knots of 0-6km shear\ncould provide enough storm organization for a damaging wind\npotential, while stretched low-level hodographs and sufficient\n0-1km SRH suggest a lower-end tornado threat for ESE-moving\nstorms. Machine-learning guidance does support these\nassertions. Also, the same favorable heavy-rainfall ingredients\nfrom the previous night will continue, extending the flash \nflood threat into Sunday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nDiurnal stabilization should begin to limit convective coverage\nand intensity late Sunday evening, with a few showers lingering\ninto Monday with one last shortwave. By this point, PWAT will \nbe dropping off thanks to developing drier NW flow, which should\nhelp to taper the heavy rainfall threat. High pressure building\nin from the Great Lakes should end precipitation by Monday\nevening. Dry weather is then favored until Wednesday, when the\nnext front could potentially arrive, although this timing could\nchange.",
